      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Powers_Lake%2C_North_Dakota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Jim Hill named the lake—and the town—Powers Lake in honor of his friend John J.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.powerslakenorthdakota.com/our-history-index-maple",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Before 1910, Powers Lake was part of Ward County; Burke County was created on July 15, 1910, and the town joined it.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Powers_Lake%2C_North_Dakota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Great Northern Railway’s branch line toward Canada sparked Powers Lake’s birth, and a grain elevator built then still stands beside the old right‑of‑way.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Powers_Lake%2C_North_Dakota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In the early 1890s, the Power family established a sheep ranch on the south shore of the lake before the town was founded.",
